17] [17592186383412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81eab9b6-2c22-56c2-8b09-bb7ba88bb023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383413 #edb/ref 1080 "$4,$ $3,$ $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383413 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383413 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45fdf803-5c63-5fd1-98ac-6d33e693cf05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383414 #edb/ref 1080 "$4,$ $4,$ $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383414 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383414 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b43b2a5-0f26-58bc-89b2-44f5ea0b5aee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383415 #edb/ref 1080 "$3,$ $2,$ $1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383415 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383415 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e057365e-ebcb-5deb-8291-d5e0f828271e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383416 #edb/ref 1080 "$2,$ $2,$ $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383416 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8afd4451-cbc1-5888-a4c6-5aa0879d00a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)y(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1956c8a5-a177-5c5c-b051-b1da508f8665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385241 #edb/ref 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy+y \\\\ -2x-y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+y)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+y)=x+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x-y)=-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x+1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "afde4ae9-9caa-51ba-9a0b-0717e063dff7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385243 #edb/ref 1080 "spiral sink" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7b0dc48-c7d4-5805-b01b-399f0655915d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385244 #edb/ref 1080 "spiral source" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385244 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385244 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db93c820-cdc5-57a8-adbc-c7e5eaa0ce4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385245 #edb/ref 1080 "saddle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385245 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385245 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d78754e4-4c07-595b-b8cb-2a97584ec3bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385246 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c3063b07-816d-50df-95b3-d097e71bd625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385247 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385247 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385247 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b740bd17-91cf-53d0-a434-4151f0f4cefc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385248 #edb/ref 1080 "asymptotically stable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385248 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385248 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6fb2a109-43b0-5f3a-aaa4-20dd1a6efaa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5716f5b5-2e23-5cd2-a381-388287b59e4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385259 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}+3L\\{y\\}=L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\}+L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)+3Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s)-1 & =\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+3)Y(s) & =1+\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+4}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{s+2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}+s+6}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}+s+6=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+3)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(-3)^{2}+(-3)+6=A((-3)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,12=13A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n1=A+B\\,⟹\\,1=\\frac{12}{13}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n6=4A+3C\\,⟹\\,6=4(\\frac{12}{13})+3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{10}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{(\\frac{12}{13})}{13(s+3)}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{13}s+\\frac{10}{13})}{{s}^{2}+4}=\\frac{12}{13(s+3)}+\\frac{s+10}{13({s}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}\\} & ={e}^{-3t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{12}{13}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}\\}+\\frac{1}{13}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+\\frac{5}{13}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{12}{13}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref